  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/81c20d212bc5d364e08c7ebb1898d648e702efded1649bef981c77e6fe493d4c.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{\u2032}(4).$\n\n${f}^{\u2032}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To compute ${f}^{\u2032}(4),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in [3,5].$ Notice that $x=4$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/19529aed824e49ee92fdd18ddef633114d271b71b776f90d127afe5125641a40.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (3,5)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u0394f}{\u0394x} & =\\frac{5-(-1)}{5-3}=\\frac{6}{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(4)=3.$",
